
How codegraph works
A TypeScript CLI + MCP server (69k★) that builds a symbol-level knowledge graph in SQLite (.codegraph/codegraph.db, WAL mode, FTS5). It's 100% local.
Pipeline (src/extraction/ → src/db/ → src/graph/traversal.ts):
1. Extraction — tree-sitter (WASM, 20+ langs) or its Rust kernel parses files into nodes (function, class, method, enum, route, component…) and edges (calls, imports, implements, references).
2. Reference resolution — links unresolved references to definitions across files (incl. dynamic-dispatch hops and cross-language bridge rules — Swift↔ObjC, React Native, Expo).
3. Storage — nodes/edges/files in SQLite; FTS5 for name search; FileRecord tracks content-hash + mtime for incremental sync.
4. Query/traversal — codegraph_explore (surgical context: source + call flow + blast-radius in one call, with dedup of code already in context), codegraph_search, codegraph_callers, codegraph_impact, codegraph_node.
5. Auto-sync — native file watcher (FSEvents/inotify) with debounced re-index + a staleness banner in responses during the debounce window + connect-time catch-up reconciliation.
6. Machine-adaptive — worker pools / caches sized from real cgroup core count + available RAM.
Key point: codegraph and Mnemonic share the same substrate — SQLite + FTS5 + incremental content-hash. The difference is what they index into it.
So: Mnemonic is "a better grep" (semantic text search over chunks); codegraph is "code intelligence" (answer who calls X, what breaks if I change X). The single missing ingredient is the symbol + edge layer.
Best parts to implement in Mnemonic (ranked)
Good news: this is an addition on top of the existing schema, not a rewrite. Mnemonic already has the storage engine, incremental hashing, per-project stores, and code_read. You mostly need a new graph layer + resolvers + three new MCP tools.
Tier 1 — the core win (do first):
1. Symbol + edge schema — add migrations: symbols(project, path, name, kind, start_line, end_line, content_hash) and edges(src_symbol, dst_symbol, relation) + FTS5 over symbol names. This unlocks everything below.
2. code_callers, code_callees, code_impact tools — trivial SQL over edges once they exist. This is the feature that makes it "code intelligence" instead of "grep."
3. A reference resolver — the engine that builds edges. Start with the 4 languages Mnemonic already indexes (go, ts, tsx, md) using heuristic/regex import+call resolution (Go is very tractable: import "path" → package → symbols). Expand to more langs later. Codegraph's insight to copy: per-language extractors behind one interface, with a verified fallback, not one giant parser.
Tier 2 — freshness & ranking (cheap, high payoff):
4. Auto-sync watcher + staleness banner — add an fsnotify watcher to skillgrid serve/mcp with debounce. The staleness half is free: Mnemonic already stores mtime_ns/content_hash, so code_read/code_search can flag files that changed since the last index and tell the agent to re-read. This directly upgrades the stale-index nudge already in the plugins.
5. deprioritize config — Mnemonic's indexing.yaml has include/exclude; add a deprioritize[] that stays indexed but never outranks first-party code in CodeSearch ordering (in fts.go code_search).
Tier 3 — polish (optional, web-heavy repos):
6. Framework-aware routes — pattern extractors that emit route nodes + references edges (Django/FastAPI/Express/Nest/Rails…). Niche but high-value for web codebases.
7. code_explore — surgical-context tool that fuses symbol lookup + a bounded neighbor hop + dedup of source already in context (codegraph's actual token-cost lever).
What to skip (architecture mismatch):
- The Rust kernel — Mnemonic is Go + modernc.org/sqlite (CGo-free). Don't port Rust; adopt the idea (byte-identical per-language extraction + parallel resolver with per-file fallback). For parsing, pick tree-sitter Go bindings for a small language set or ctags, rather than a second native runtime.
- Cross-language iOS/RN/Expo bridging — only if you actually work on those stacks.
The one-sentence takeaway: port codegraph's graph model (symbols + call/refs edges + impact analysis) as a new SQLite layer and three MCP tools on top of Mnemonic's existing chunk index, plus a debounced watcher with staleness banners — keep the Go/modernc stack, drop the Rust.
Key Learnings:
1. Mnemonic's codeindex (skillgrid-cli/internal/mnemonic/codeindex) is chunk-based FTS — files/chunks with FTS5 trigram; it indexes text windows, not symbols, so it cannot answer callers/impact.
2. codegraph and Mnemonic both use SQLite + FTS5 + content-hash incremental sync; the real gap is a symbol(node) + edge(symbol→symbol) graph layer, not the storage.
3. Mnemonic is Go + modernc.org/sqlite (CGo-free) — a Rust/WASM kernel is an architectural mismatch; tree-sitter-Go or ctags fits better.
4. Mnemonic already stores mtime_ns/content_hash per file, so a codegraph-style staleness banner on code_read/code_search is cheap to add.
5. Highest-value Mnemonic additions, in order: symbols+edges schema → code_callers/code_callees/code_impact → import/call resolver for go/ts/tsx/md → fsnotify auto-sync → deprioritize search ranking.
